object-change-auth-derived-object: TPM2_ObjectChangeAuth rejects a derivation parent

Classification: spec-deviation bug fix.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Part 3, TPM2_CreateLoaded): "If *parentHandle*
references a Derivation Parent or a Primary Seed, then *outPrivate* will be an
Empty Buffer." with the note "Returning *outPrivate* would imply that the
returned primary or derived object can be loaded, and it cannot. It can only
be re-derived."; Part 3, TPM2_ObjectChangeAuth: "This command may not be used
to change the authorization value for an NV Index or a Primary Object."

TPM2_ObjectChangeAuth of a Derived Object (created by TPM2_CreateLoaded under
a Derivation Parent) protects the new sensitive area with the Derivation
Parent, a KEYEDHASH object without a symmetric definition: the TCG reference
reads an uninitialized TPMT_SYM_DEF_OBJECT (CryptoCommon_SelectSymDefObject()
fails for it) and may crash, ibmswtpm2 reinterprets the KEYEDHASH scheme as a
symmetric definition. A Derived Object cannot be loaded, so no private area may
be returned for it; the specification gives no response code, so the patch
returns TPM_RC_TYPE + RC_ObjectChangeAuth_parentHandle (the code for a
parentHandle that cannot be used as the parent), as tpm-rs does. Found by
differential fuzzing.
diff --git a/src/ObjectCommands.c b/src/ObjectCommands.c
--- a/src/ObjectCommands.c
+++ b/src/ObjectCommands.c
@@ -522,6 +522,11 @@
 			 &object->name, &QNCompare);
     if(!MemoryEqual2B(&object->qualifiedName.b, &QNCompare.b))
 	return TPM_RCS_TYPE + RC_ObjectChangeAuth_parentHandle;
+    // tpm-rs differential testing -- C-ref spec deviation fix (NOT determinism):
+    // a Derived Object cannot be loaded, so its Derivation Parent (which has no
+    // symmetric definition) cannot protect a new private area for it.
+    if(HandleToObject(in->parentHandle)->attributes.derivation == SET)
+	return TPM_RCS_TYPE + RC_ObjectChangeAuth_parentHandle;
     // Command Output
     // Prepare the sensitive area with the new authorization value
     sensitive = object->sensitive;
